<exercises xml:id="s_real_tuples_ex">

<exercisegroup>
<title>Tuple building</title>


  <introduction>
    <p>
      For the given index set <m>I</m> and tuple indexed by <m>I</m>, display the tuple using the appropriate notational conventions introduced in <xref ref="ss_tuples"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>I=\{1,2,3,4,5\}</m>, <m>\bolda=(\cos(\pi j/2))_{j\in I}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>I=\{1,2,3,4\}\times \{1,2,3\}</m>, <m>A=(i+j)_{(i,j)\in I}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>I=\Z_+</m>, <m>s=(2^k)_{k\in I}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>I=\{(i,j)\mid 1\leq i,j\leq 3\}</m>, <m>A=[2]_{(i,j)\in I}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>I=\{(i,j)\mid 1\leq i,j\leq 5\}</m>, <m>A=[a_{i,j}]_{(i,j)\in I}</m>, where 
<md>
  a_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
  1\amp \text{if } \abs{i-j}&gt; 1\\
  -1\amp \text{if } \abs{i-j}\leq 1
  \end{cases}
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
<exercisegroup>
<title>Tuple equality</title>


  <introduction>
    <p>
      Explain why the given tuples are not equal. Your justification must refer to a specific condition of <xref ref="d_tuple_equality_real"/>, <xref ref="prop_ntuple_equality"/>, or <xref ref="prop_equality_matrix"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>\boldx=(1,2,3)</m>, <m>\boldy=\rowvec{1\amp 2\amp 3}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
  <statement>
  <p>
  <m>\bolda=(1,2,3)</m>, <m>\boldb=(1,3,3)</m>
  </p>
  </statement>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>\boldx=(1,1,1,1)</m>, <m>\boldy=(1,1,1,1,1)</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
  <statement>
  <p>
  <m>A=\begin{amatrix}
  2\amp 2 \\ 2\amp 2
\end{amatrix}</m>, <m>B=\begin{amatrix}
2 \amp 2\amp 2\\ 2\amp 2\amp 2
\end{amatrix}</m>
  </p>
  </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
  <statement>
  <p>
  <m>s=(1,2,1,\dots)</m>, <m>t=(1,1,2,1,\dots)</m>
  </p>
  </statement>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
<exercisegroup>
<title>Vector operations of <m>\R^I</m></title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Compute the given expressions using the vector operations of the relevant vector space <m>\R^I</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
       Let 
       <md>
        <mrow>\bolda \amp = (-2,0,1,3) \amp \boldb\amp =(0,0,1,2) \amp \boldc\amp=(4,3,2,1)</mrow>
       </md>. 
       <ol marker="(a)" cols="3">
        <li>
          <p>
          <m>(-2)\boldb</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>3\bolda+2\boldb-2\boldc</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>0\bolda+2\boldb+3\boldc</m>
          </p>
        </li>
       </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
  <statement>
  <p>
  Let 
  <md>
    <mrow>A \amp = \begin{amatrix}[rrr]
    1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
    4 \amp 5 \amp 6
    \end{amatrix} \amp B \amp = \begin{amatrix}[rrr]
    -3 \amp -3 \amp -2 \\
    0 \amp 1 \amp 1
    \end{amatrix}
    \amp C \amp = \begin{amatrix}[rrr]
    1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
    0 \amp 1 \amp 0
    \end{amatrix}
  </mrow>
  </md>.
  <ol marker="(a)" cols="3">
  <li>
    <p>
     <m>A-B</m> 
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <m>7C</m>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <m>-A-B-C</m>
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
</p>
  </statement>
  </exercise>
 <exercise>
 <statement>
 <p>
 Let 
 <md>
  <mrow>s \amp = (s_i)_{i=1}^\infty \amp t\amp =(t_i)_{i=1}^\infty \amp u\amp=(1,1,1,\dots)</mrow>
 </md>,
 where 
 <md>
  <mrow>s_i \amp =\begin{cases}
  1\amp \text{if } i \text{ is odd} \\
  2\amp \text{if } i\text{ is even}
  \end{cases} \amp 
t_i\amp= \begin{cases}
  1\amp \text{if } i \text{ is even} \\
  2\amp \text{if } i\text{ is odd}
  \end{cases}
 </mrow>
 </md>.
 Express your answers below as expanded infinite sequences with enough terms provided to see a pattern. 
<ol marker="(a)" cols="3">
  <li>
    <p>
      <m>-s+t</m>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <m>(-3)t</m>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <m>s+t-3u</m>
    </p>
  </li>
</ol> 
</p>
 </statement>
 </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Show that every element of <m>\R^n</m> can be written as a linear combination of <m>\{\bolde_1,\bolde_2,\dots, \bolde_n\}</m>. In other words, every element of <m>\R^n</m> is a linear combination of the standard basis vectors of <m>\R^n</m>. 
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Show that every element of <m>\ML_{m,n}(\R)</m> can be written as a linear combination of <m>\{ E_{i,j}\mid 1\leq i\leq m, 1\leq j\leq n\}</m>. In other words, every element of <m>\ML_{m,n}(\R)</m> is a linear combination of the standard basis matrices of <m>\ML_{m,n}(\R)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Show that it is not the case that every element of <m>\R^\infty</m> can be written as a linear combination of the set <m>S=\{\bolde_1,\bolde_2,\bolde_3,\dots\}</m> of indicator vectors of <m>\R^\infty</m>. In more detail, provide an explicit infinite sequence <m>s=(s_i)_{i=1}^\infty</m> that is not a linear combination of <m>S</m>. 
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
  <p>
    Show that any linear combination of <m>S</m> is of the form <m>\sum_{i=1}^n c_i\bolde_i</m> for some positive integer <m>n</m> and scalars <m>c_1,c_2,\dots, c_n</m> (some of which could be zero). Expand out this linear combination as an infinite sequence and provide an infinite sequence that doesn't look like this! 
  </p>
</hint>
<solution>
  <p>
    The underlying issue here is that by definition a linear combination of any set of vectors is a <em>finite</em> sum of scalar multiples of vectors from that set. In the present case, that means an arbitrary linear combination of <m>S</m> is of the form 
    <md>
      s=d_{i_1}\bolde_{i_1}+d_{i_2}\bolde_{i_2}+\cdots +d_{i_k}\bolde_{i_k}
    </md>
    for some positive integers <m>i_1&lt; i_2&lt; \dots &lt; i_k</m> and scalars <m>d_{i_k}\in \R</m>. Letting <m>n=i_k</m> and setting 
    <md>
      c_i=\begin{cases}
      d_{i_j}\amp \text{if } i=i_j \text{ for some } 1\leq j\leq k \\
      0\amp \text{otherwise}
      \end{cases}
    </md>,
    we have 
    <md>
      s=c_1\bolde_1+c_2\bolde_2+\cdots +c_n\bolde_n
    </md>. 
    This shows that any linear combination of <m>S</m> can be expressed in the form <m>s=\sum_{i=1}^nc_i\bolde_i</m>, as claimed in the hint. Now since by definition <m>\bolde_i</m> is the infinite sequence 
    <md number="yes">
      <mrow>\bolde_i \amp =(0,0,\dots, 0, \underset{i\text{-th term}}{1},0,\dots)</mrow>
    </md>
    we have 
    <md>
      \sum_{i=1}^nc_i\bolde_i=(c_1,c_2,\dots, c_n,0,0,\dots)
    </md>.
    This shows that any linear combination of the <m>\bolde_i</m> is a sequence with only finitely many nonzero terms! In particular the infinite sequence 
    <md number="yes">
      <mrow>(1)_{i=1}^\infty \amp =(1,1,\dots)</mrow>
    </md>
    is not a linear combination of the <m>\bolde_i</m>. 
  </p>
</solution>
</exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Finish the proof of <xref ref="th_tuples_vector_space"/>. That is, show that the vector operations of <m>\R^I</m> satisfy Axioms i, v, vii, and viii of <xref ref="d_vector_space"/>.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Finish the proof of <xref ref="th_zero_space"/>. That is, show that the unique operations defined satisfy Axioms i-ii, and v-viii of <xref ref="d_vector_space"/>.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>


<exercise>
  <title>True or false</title>
  
  
<statement>
<p>
True or false: if true, provide a proof; if false, provide an explicit counterexample. 
<ol>
  <li>
    <p>
      If <m>I</m>-tuples <m>(a_i)_{i\in I}</m> and <m>(b_i)_{i\in I}</m> have the same set of values (see <xref ref="d_tuple_real"/>), then <m>(a_i)_{i\in I}=(b_i)_{i\in I}</m>. 
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Every element of <m>\R^2</m> can be written as a linear combination of the vectors <m>(1,1)</m> and <m>(1,0)</m>.  
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Every element of <m>\ML_{2,2}(\R)</m> can be written as a linear combination of the matrices 
      <md>
        <mrow>A \amp =\begin{amatrix}[cc]
        1\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1
        \end{amatrix} \amp B=\begin{amatrix}[cc]
        0\amp 0\\ 1\amp 0
        \end{amatrix}</mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      If <m>I</m> is finite, then every element of <m>\R^I</m> can be written as a linear combination of the indicator vectors <m>\bolde_i</m>, <m>i\in I</m>. 
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      If <m>V</m> is a vector space, then <m>V</m> is infinite. 
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>






</exercises>
